  <ArticleHero byline="By the Cliffco editorial team">P&amp;L vs Bank Statement Loans, and What a "WVOE Loan" Really Is</ArticleHero>

  <ArticleBody>

    <p class="mt-8 font-bold">For the Business Owner Who Needs a Cleaner Path to Qualification</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If you own a business and assembling two years of tax returns sounds like a project you don't have time for, there are lighter documentation paths built for exactly that. Two of them are real programmes: a 12-month P&amp;L loan and a bank statement loan. A third thing you'll see advertised, the "WVOE loan", isn't a loan programme at all, and it's worth understanding why before you go looking for one.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">First, What a WVOE Actually Is</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A WVOE is a Written Verification of Employment, Fannie Mae Form 1005. Your <em>employer</em> fills it in, confirming your job, your dates of employment and your pay. A verbal verification, or VVOE, is the short version, usually done in the days before closing to confirm you're still working there.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">It's a way of documenting income, not a product you apply for. On a full documentation loan a WVOE can sit alongside your pay stubs and W-2s, or sometimes stand in for them. That's the whole of it.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">You'll still see "WVOE loan" used as shorthand for a lightly documented loan for a W-2 employee. The reason it doesn't help a business owner is simple: a WVOE is completed by an employer about an employee. If you own the business, there's no independent employer to complete one, and no lender will let you verify your own income on a form. If you're self-employed, the two programmes below are the ones built for you.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How a 12-Month P&amp;L Loan Works</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">This one qualifies you on a single current profit and loss statement instead of tax returns. You provide the most recent 12 months, and the end date has to be less than 60 days old when you close, so it's a genuine snapshot of where the business is now.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">That matters if your returns are behind your reality. A slower year two years ago drags a two-year average down; a current P&amp;L doesn't average you backwards.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The conditions are specific, and worth knowing before you start:</p>

    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
      <li>The P&amp;L must be prepared by an independent CPA, IRS Enrolled Agent or CTEC preparer, and <strong>that same preparer must have filed your most recent business tax returns</strong>. A preparer who is related to you or involved in your business doesn't qualify.</li>
      <li>Both you and the preparer sign it. <strong>A P&amp;L you prepare yourself is never permitted</strong>, under any circumstances.</li>
      <li>You need at least two years of self-employment in your current profession, and you must own at least 50% of the business.</li>
      <li>Your business bank statements are used to check the P&amp;L. Gross revenue on it has to land within 10% of your qualified deposits, so a statement that doesn't reconcile creates questions rather than answering them.</li>
      <li>Qualifying income is the <strong>lower</strong> of the P&amp;L figure or the income you put on your initial application, so the application is worth getting right the first time.</li>
      <li>The programme runs to 80% loan-to-value.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How a Bank Statement Loan Works</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here your deposits do the talking. You provide 12 or 24 consecutive months of statements, and a share of your gross deposits becomes your qualifying income.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">That share is the part people get wrong. It isn't a flat 50%. The expense ratio is set by what your business does and how many people it employs, so a solo consultant and a product business with a warehouse are not treated alike. A lender who quotes you a single blanket percentage before understanding your business is guessing.</p>

    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
      <li>Statements must be consecutive and the most recent available. Fewer than 12 months isn't eligible.</li>
      <li>Up to three businesses or five accounts can be used. Beyond three businesses you'll need to use a personal account.</li>
      <li>A business narrative is required, covering what the business does, where it operates, how many people it employs and its cost of goods.</li>
      <li>Qualifying income is multiplied by your documented ownership percentage, and is again the <strong>lower</strong> of the calculated figure or the income on your initial application.</li>
      <li>If you're using 24 months and your income has declined, the most recent 12 months are what count.</li>
      <li><strong>If you're paid on a 1099 by a single company, this path isn't open to you</strong> — that profile qualifies as full documentation instead.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How to Choose Between Them</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">It comes down to which one tells your story more accurately, not which is lighter.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If your business has grown recently and a current P&amp;L shows meaningfully more than your returns do, the P&amp;L route captures that growth. If your deposits are strong and steady but your accounting is informal, or you'd rather not involve a preparer, bank statements build the case from money that actually arrived.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">One practical tiebreaker: the P&amp;L programme depends on a preparer who already files your business returns. If you don't have that relationship, bank statements are the faster road.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Check whether your current performance is meaningfully stronger than your last two returns show.</strong> If it is, the P&amp;L route is the one that captures it.</li>
      <li><strong>Find out whether the preparer who files your business returns can prepare and sign a P&amp;L.</strong> The programme requires that specific person, so this is the step that decides whether it's available to you at all.</li>
      <li><strong>Pull 12 months of business bank statements and work out your average monthly deposits.</strong> That figure, less an expense ratio set by your business type and headcount, is roughly where bank statement qualifying income lands.</li>
      <li><strong>If you're paid 1099 by one company, stop and talk to a loan officer first.</strong> Bank statement qualification isn't open to that profile, and you'll save yourself gathering documents you can't use.</li>
      <li><strong>Make sure your initial application income matches what you'll document.</strong> Both programmes qualify you on the lower of the two, so an optimistic number on the application can cap you below what your business supports.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Find the Right Documentation Path for Your Profile</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Cliffco's Self-Employed Income Calculator can help you evaluate which documentation approach produces the strongest qualifying income for your situation. Bring your most recent P&amp;L or a deposit estimate and see where the numbers land before you commit to a product or a lender.</p>

    <!-- Embedded lite Self-Employed Income Calculator -->
    <div class="mt-12 border-2 border-[var(--color-ink)] overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-white px-6 py-5 border-b-2 border-[var(--color-ink)]">
        <p class="eyebrow text-xs mb-1">Quick check</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Compare your qualifying income.</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">See how conventional lenders vs. bank statement lenders view your income, side by side.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <label for="art-se-y1" class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Year 1 net income, per tax return ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-y1" type="number" value="80000" min="0" step="1000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="art-se-y2" class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Year 2 net income, per tax return ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-y2" type="number" value="85000" min="0" step="1000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="art-se-dep" class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual depreciation add-back ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-dep" type="number" value="5000" min="0" step="500" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="art-se-add" class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Other add-backs ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-add" type="number" value="0" min="0" step="500" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="art-se-dep2" class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">12-month bank statement deposits ($)</label>
            <input id="art-se-dep2" type="number" value="180000" min="0" step="1000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label for="art-se-ratio" class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Business expense ratio (%)</label>
            <input id="art-se-ratio" type="number" value="40" min="0" max="90" step="5" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="art-se-btn" class="btn btn-primary mt-5">Compare My Income</button>

        <div id="art-se-result" class="hidden mt-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-black/15 ">
            <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Conventional Qualifying Income</p>
                <p id="art-se-conv" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">per month</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Bank Statement Qualifying Income</p>
                <p id="art-se-bank" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">per month</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Recommended Path</p>
                <p id="art-se-rec" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">Conventional path uses 24-month average (or lower year if declining). Bank statement path uses deposits minus expense ratio, annualized. Both are estimates. Actual lender guidelines vary.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-4 p-5 bg-[var(--color-ink)] flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold">Not sure which path fits your income?</p>
              <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-1">A Cliffco self-employed specialist can run both scenarios with real lender guidelines and tell you what you qualify for.</p>
            </div>
            <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-ink)] shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap">Talk to a loan officer</a>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-center text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Want to model other scenarios? <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/self-employed-income/" class="text-[var(--color-ink)] font-medium">View the full self-employed income calculator →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </ArticleBody>
</BlogLayout>

<style>
  .art-calc-input {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-soft-gray, #d1d5db);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: white;
    outline: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .art-calc-input:focus {
    border-color: var(--color-ink);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-ink) 15%, transparent);
  }
</style>

<script>

  /**
   * Read a number box, honouring the bounds the markup declares.
   *
   * Without this, a rate of 1e9 overflowed the amortisation factor to NaN, a ratio past its
   * maximum produced negative qualifying income, and a negative price printed "$-0". Money
   * boxes declare no maximum, which is correct - a large number in gives a large number out.
   */
  function num(id: string): number {
    const el = document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement | null;
    if (!el) return 0;
    const raw = parseFloat(el.value);
    if (!Number.isFinite(raw)) return 0;
    const max = parseFloat(el.max);
    return Math.min(Math.max(raw, 0), Number.isFinite(max) ? max : Infinity);
  }

  const seBtn = document.getElementById("art-se-btn")!;
  seBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const y1 = num("art-se-y1");
    const y2 = num("art-se-y2");
    const dep = num("art-se-dep");
    const add = num("art-se-add");
    const deposits = num("art-se-dep2");
    const ratio = num("art-se-ratio");

    const convAnnual = y2 < y1
      ? y2 + dep + add
      : ((y1 + y2) / 2) + dep + add;
    const convMonthly = convAnnual / 12;
    const bankMonthly = (deposits * (1 - ratio / 100)) / 12;
    const fmt = (v: number) => (v < 0 ? "-$" : "$") + Math.abs(Math.round(v)).toLocaleString("en-US");

    (document.getElementById("art-se-conv") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(convMonthly);
    (document.getElementById("art-se-bank") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(bankMonthly);

    const recEl = document.getElementById("art-se-rec") as HTMLElement;
    if (bankMonthly > convMonthly * 1.1) recEl.textContent = "Bank Statement";
    else if (convMonthly > bankMonthly * 1.1) recEl.textContent = "Conventional";
    else recEl.textContent = "Compare both";

    (document.getElementById("art-se-result") as HTMLElement).classList.remove("hidden");
  });
</script>
